<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martin Luther</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1483">1483</a>–<a href="/years/1546">1546</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martin Luther was a German theologian and monk whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a> of 1517 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>. Born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a> in the County of Mansfeld, he refused to recant before Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> in 1521. Protected by Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Wise</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, he translated the Bible into German, and his teachings became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="Martin Luther" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d.%C3%84._-_Martin_Luther,_1528_(Veste_Coburg).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zürich Bible, printed by Christoph Froschauer, was completed in 1531, three years before <b>Martin Luther</b>’s complete German Bible appeared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The court culture of the last Griffins is known through objects such as the Croÿ Tapestry of 1554, showing the dynasty with <b>Martin Luther</b>, and the Pomeranian Art Cabinet made in Augsburg for Duke Philip II, together with Eilhard Lubinus’s great map of the duchy of 1618.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The electorate’s greatest impact on world history was the protection it gave <b>Martin Luther</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>This fragmentation allowed the Reformation, launched by <b>Martin Luther</b> at Wittenberg in 1517, to survive where a centralised monarchy might have crushed it, and it encouraged intellectual competition among rival courts and universities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The movement that followed created, a century before <b>Martin Luther</b>, a church that rejected papal authority in practice and won legal recognition through the Compacts of Basel in 1436.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach, elected in 1511, fought a last war against Poland in 1519–1521, then followed <b>Martin Luther</b>'s advice to secularise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.52,11.55&amp;year=1523" aria-label="Eisleben on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 380 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Martin Luther"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 650h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 650h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.52,11.55&amp;year=1523">What was here in 1523? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Our Lord and Master Jesus Christ, when He said Poenitentiam agite, willed that the whole life of believers should be repentance.&#8221;</p><footer>Ninety-five Theses, thesis 1 (1517) &#183; tr. Adolph Spaeth, L. D. Reed, H. E. Jacobs et al., 1915 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/274/pg274-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A Christian man is the most free lord of all, and subject to none; a Christian man is the most dutiful servant of all, and subject to every one.&#8221;</p><footer>Concerning Christian Liberty (1520) &#183; tr. R. S. Grignon, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1911/pg1911-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A mighty fortress is our God, A bulwark never failing; Our helper he, amid the flood Of mortal ills prevailing.&#8221;</p><footer>A Mighty Fortress Is Our God (hymn) &#183; tr. Frederic Henry Hedge, 1853 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Army_and_Navy_Hymnal/Hymns/A_Mighty_Fortress_is_Our_God" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Good works do not make a good man, but a good man does good works…&#8221;</p><footer>Concerning Christian Liberty (1520) &#183; tr. R. S. Grignon, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1911/pg1911-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;One thing, and one alone, is necessary for life, justification, and Christian liberty; and that is the most holy word of God, the Gospel of Christ…&#8221;</p><footer>Concerning Christian Liberty (1520) &#183; tr. R. S. Grignon, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1911/pg1911-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I cannot bear with laws for the interpretation of the word of God, since the word of God, which teaches liberty in all other things, ought not to be bound.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Pope Leo X, prefaced to Concerning Christian Liberty (1520) &#183; tr. R. S. Grignon, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1911/pg1911-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
